{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>And he brought the second ram, the ram of consecration.</b> I have already explained (Exodus 28:41) that the matter of the millu'im is that they should have a complete grant of authority (יד שלמה) to perform the service of YHWH, and it says the same concerning the altar itself and those who serve upon it. Now all these offerings come to fill their hands, as it is written (Exodus 29:1), \"And this is the thing that you shall do to them to sanctify them to minister to Me: take one young bull and two unblemished rams.\" But the second ram is called \"the ram of consecration\" (אֵיל הַמִּלֻּאִים) because it is the last of these offerings, and through it their hands are filled and they minister before Him, may He be exalted, to indicate that all of them are indispensable for the matter. And it is possible that the sin-offering is to make atonement for the altar and to sanctify it, for so it is written (Leviticus 8:15), and the burnt-offering is for acceptance on behalf of the priests, like the atonement of any voluntary burnt-offering, and the peace-offerings are a thanksgiving to HaShem for having given them, in His house and within His walls, a hand and a name. Now the peace-offerings alone come for the filling of the hands, and therefore they are called millu'im. And it says \"millu'im\" and not milluy, because the fats and the thigh and also the meal offering — all are brought near."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:51:50.348584878Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}